    "content": {
        "title": "Air quality data for NYC – Environment & Health Data Portal",
        "headingText": [
            "Air quality",
            "Air Pollution and Health",
            "To Reduce Health Risks",
            "About Neighborhood Air Quality",
            "Related data on:",
            "Keywords:",
            "Change topic",
            "About Air quality",
            "Air Pollution and Health",
            "To Reduce Health Risks",
            "About Neighborhood Air Quality",
            "About neighborhood boundaries",
            "About trend charts",
            "Available time periods",
            "About measures",
            "About correlation",
            "How calculated",
            "Save this visualization",
            "Fine particles (PM 2.5)",
            "Concentration of fine particles in the air. Fine particles are emitted by vehicles, building boilers, and other combustion — and are a major form of air pollution that harms health. Neighborhood sensors measure PM2.5 at the street-level, where people are most active. These sensors are designed to let us measure differences in air pollution over time and locations within New York City.",
            "Black carbon",
            "Concentration of black carbon in the air. Black carbon is a major component of fine particles, which contribute to both short-term health effects and chronic illness",
            "Nitric oxide (NO)",
            "Concentration of nitric oxide in the air. Nitric oxide is formed by combusion, causes breathing problems, and contributes to smog and acid rain.",
            "Nitrogen dioxide (NO2)",
            "Concentration of nitrogen dioxide in the air. Nitrogen dioxide is a pollutant formed by combustion. It causes breathing problems and contributes to smog and acid rain.",
            "Ozone (O3)",
            "Concentration of ozone in the air. Ozone is a common air pollutant that can harm breathing and worsen respiratory conditions.",
            "Sulfur dioxide (SO2)",
            "Concentration of sulfur dioxide in the air. Sulfur dioxide comes from burning some kinds of fuel oil. It can worsen lung diseases.",
            "Regulatory PM2.5 monitoring",
            "Concentration of fine particles in the air. Fine particles are emitted by vehicles, building boilers, and other combustion - and are a major form of air pollution that harms health. Regulatory PM2.5 sensors collect readings on the tops of buildings that measure regional changes in air quality for the federal Clean Air Act. This indicator is one of the federal standards for PM2.5 (9.0 µg/m3).",
            "Air quality action days",
            "Days when air quality is unhealthy. Action days for sensitive groups are counted when either ozone or PM2.5 levels are above 100, and for the general population when levels are above 150.",
            "Fine particles (PM 2.5) action days",
            "Days when PM2.5 is at an unhealthy level. PM2.5 is emitted by combustion and are a major form of air pollution that harms health. PM2.5 action days are when the AQI is above 100, mainly due to PM2.5",
            "Ozone action days",
            "Days when ozone levels are at unhealthy levels. Ozone is a common air pollutant that can harm breathing and worsen asthma and other respiratory conditions. Ozone action days are when the AQI is above 100, mainly due to ozone.",
            "You might also be interested in:",
            "More related to:",
            "Take action",
            "Page Footer",
            "Search"
        ],
